Polymorphism means:
“One thing can behave in many different ways.”

The word comes from:
Poly → Many
Morph → Forms
So, polymorphism = many forms.

Real Life Example
Think about a person.
A single person can behave differently:
-->At home → son/daughter
-->At office → employee
-->With friends → friend
Same person → different behavior.

USES :
1.reuse code
2.reduce duplicate code
3.make programs flexible
4.change behavior easily

Types of Polymorphism :
1.Compile Time Polymorphism
Method Overloading
2.Run Time Polymorphism
Method Overriding

1. Compile Time Polymorphism
(Method Overloading)
Meaning:
Same method name
but different parameters.
Java decides which method to call during compilation.

Example code for Compile time :

class Calculator {

    void add(int a, int b) {
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }
    void add(int a, int b, int c) {
        System.out.println(a + b + c);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator c = new Calculator();
        c.add(10, 20);
        c.add(10, 20, 30);
    }
}

Output :
30
60

2. Run Time Polymorphism
(Method Overriding)
Meaning :
Child class changes the method behavior of parent class.
Java decides which method to call during runtime.

Example code for Runtime : 

class Animal 
{
    void sound() 
    {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal 
{
    void sound() 
    {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        a.sound();
    }
}

Output :
Dog barks

Difference Between Overloading and Overriding :
| Feature            | Overloading  | Overriding     |
| ------------------ | ------------ | -------------- |
| Same Method Name   | Yes          | Yes            |
| Parameters         | Different    | Same           |




Encapsulation in Java (OOP Concept):
Wrapping data and methods into a single unit and 
protecting the data from direct access.

In Java:

-->variables → hidden using private
-->access given using:
-->getter methods
-->setter methods
-->Hide data → Allow controlled access

Encapsulation is Achieved in Java by Using:
1.private variables
2.public getter and setter methods

example code : 

class Student {
    // Private variable
    private int marks;

    // Setter method
    void setMarks(int m) {
        marks = m;
    }

    // Getter method
    int getMarks() {
        return marks;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student obj = new Student();
        obj.setMarks(90);
        System.out.println(obj.getMarks());
    }
}

Output :
90
























